Complete project read accounting for the generic metadata entries
================================================================

This is a static accounting argument for the new generic Source, not an
execution result or a physical-disk/RAM benchmark. Core8cdc is unchanged.
The old mathematical workers retain all their own read and work bounds.
All bodies mentioned below are exact descriptor-bound project files.
Python's standard library, platform/process observation and OS page-cache
traffic are outside this logical project body-byte measure, as in the
retained Source/IO convention. No claim about their physical IO is made.

Let S be the active entry manifest's complete Source_with_invocation_reserve.
The manifest includes the entry, common, helper, lexical parser, every loaded
operation module, Core policy/profile/base guard and Root recorder. S is at
most131072. The active module lists are fixed by the eight wrapper bodies.

Outside the child Ledger, the wrapper reads common once; common reads and
compiles the helper, lexical parser and policy once; the wrapper reads each
listed operation module once. The outer profile reads its policy once and
its base twice. Its interpreter consumes its own entry once and the profile
also hashes that entry once. Each Source body is thus used no more than
three times outside the Ledger under the literal fixed call graph. Charging
3S is a conservative bound, including a full nominal entry/Source allowance
and the invocation reserve. It is separate from the manifest Source reads
and closing reads which the child Ledger itself records.

The outer profile also reads each of these descriptor-bound documents once:

  budget, launch, Source peer, actual Core control leaf, active role manifest.

Finally preflight_targets reads each explicitly existing inventory target
once. Repeated identical descriptors in that inventory are charged at their
actual row multiplicity rather than deduplicated. The profile's other
launch/evidence/inventory validators perform no file-body reads. Therefore
read_prepaid returns

  P = 3S + sum(five outer document byte sizes)
         + sum(all existing inventory row byte sizes).

Source-path stat/hash reads made through the child Ledger are not subtracted
from P; this deliberately counts their duplicate module-load copies as well.
No recursive historical review-body closure is claimed. Root must compute
this prepayment from the exact approved budget and manifest BEFORE invoking
the profile, and own the full read bound in its launch. The child recomputes
it independently once the genuine started marker supplies the exact launch.
A malformed/refused launch earns no positive output or bounded-success claim;
the unchanged Core still limits each descriptor and the inventory components.

The child sets Ledger.read_limit to64MiB-P and checks its already loaded
prefix against that limit. Every first Ledger read reserves twice its body
size, including its later closing read. Cached returns do not read a body
again. Closing reads validate the entire recorded old signature and the
fixed old length at most4MiB BEFORE read(old_length+1); a grown file cannot
select a new unbounded read size. Closing also retains the whole body/EOF
and post-read identity checks.

Let F be all first Ledger body bytes at emit, W the serialized planned output
bytes and C the sum, for canonical replacements, of each frozen-old size plus
its new body size. The final prospective bound is

  R = P + 2F + 2W + 2C <=64MiB.

The first and closing Ledger passes cost2F. Core write_files can read an
existing equal output once during preflight and each output once on final
readback, costing at most2W. The fixed CAS reads each old body twice, each
fresh stage once and each resulting canonical body once, costing2C. Stage
readback already charged in W is not removed; conservative duplication is
intentional. All planned output and CAS bodies are admitted before the first
write. No unbounded read callback is used. The production source checks R
before Ledger.close/publication, preserves the existing path limit and emits
bootstrap_outer_read_upper=P and total_project_read_upper=R together with
its actual first_closing_input_bytes=2F. These totals are not labelled as an
independent measurement of outer process IO.

Fresh synthetic controls exercise the actual read_prepaid/read_upper
arithmetic and the actual closing_read pre-read refusal, using tiny metadata
and a read callback. They do not simulate a genuine SDK, observe filesystem
races, enforce a kernel quota or replace Root's exact launch ownership.
